<item>
            <title>[IGNITE-16416] Unreadable result on cluster deactivation via IgniteMXBean</title>
                <link>https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/IGNITE-16416</link>
                <project id="12315922" key="IGNITE">Ignite</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;Several MX bean methods throw &lt;em&gt;IgniteException / JMException&lt;/em&gt;. Such exceptions or their causes cannot be deserialized on the client-side without class transfer or class path preparation. The error could look like:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;code panel&quot; style=&quot;border-width: 1px;&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;codeContent panelContent&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;code-java&quot;&gt;
java.rmi.UnmarshalException / java.lang.ClassNofFoundException
&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;instead of the desired exception. See the pictures attached. They represent results on jconsole. &lt;br/&gt;
The method examples:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class=&quot;code panel&quot; style=&quot;border-width: 1px;&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;codeContent panelContent&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;code-java&quot;&gt;
IgniteMXBean.clusterState(...)
IgniteMXBean.executeTask(...)
IgniteClusterMXBean.tag(...)
&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The simplest solution could be just throwing RuntimeException with the error message (example on the picture &quot;Good&quot;). &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Or we could deprecate exception-throwing deactivation methods and bring new ones returning a string like &quot;Cluster deactivated.&quot; / &quot;Cluster activated.&quot; or &quot;Failed. Cause: ...&quot;. But IgniteClusterMXBean already has several deprecated cluster-state methods.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;ins&gt;This ticket suggests another approach:&lt;/ins&gt;&lt;br/&gt;
Let&apos;s fix existing wrapping with &lt;em&gt;JMException&lt;/em&gt; so that it keeps only the error message without non-deserializable causes. Let&apos;s wrap &lt;em&gt;IgniteException/IgniteCheckedException&lt;/em&gt; into such &lt;em&gt;JMException&lt;/em&gt; in other places. This would slightly change the API: several mx-bean methods like &lt;em&gt;IgniteMXBean.clusterState(...)&lt;/em&gt; will obtain &lt;em&gt;throws JMException&lt;/em&gt;. User code might not be compiled. But why using mx bean API?&lt;/p&gt;</description>
